egin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of the Euclidean plane ${R}^{2}$ and the unit square spanned by the vectors. Computing the images of ${i,j}$ under $A$, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{′} & =Ai=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=j \\\\ {j}^{′} & =Aj=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain the following diagram:\n\n![](images/f1/f154267ed0a528b1e9fbec40064b090560410fe76dab00070e13fb3d1978b59c.png)\n\nSo, the matrix represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians counterclockwise about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ counterclockwise by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/b4/b4ded02707568b62cfa06f53ffe79b564986b4ddd4ceb7709e8aded7f1a2106b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b4df33ff-e04e-55ec-b273-9eceff7db53b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rotation Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d473b8ea-8d04-5ece-95ea-65e737aef3a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f4/f4d4d52cb24bd1e3dd4bc37736533693ff89e6b08f495f8541f23abde995a5b9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394292} {:answer/id #uuid "03ae5c24-7a66-5265-969a-3f12d946ddaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/979157d993a86617fabf4b2dfacc6d4c1d973885b42be081b4bcffb36058e091.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394293} {:answer/id #uuid "47c3f52d-cad6-592b-8617-cf6147446ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4b/4bf42e3bdeb61feccd68cf4eeb9e3c4a40e8b903a5739df77c993e9aa4abc993.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394294} {:answer/id #uuid