 represents a rotation of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians **clockwise** about the origin.\n\nTherefore, in order to find $Av$, we need to rotate $v$ **clockwise** by an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ radians, as shown below.\n\n![](images/ab/ab6a3ec35954243105f9bb6c9f1f2cc21c02358f90855f9f36f0a590092eb93d.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394297 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0f2de09-e3ad-512b-8e74-3a12edf3480d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 11 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394299} {:answer/id #uuid "1fcb9a14-b5a6-5c03-9ec6-27fac7199ae9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 11 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394300} {:answer/id #uuid "9f078de7-6bf2-52c5-bbfd-2095ee4f5399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394301} {:answer/id #uuid "76983eb3-ee3b-59df-9616-86db07326c79" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394302} {:answer/id #uuid "9158d772-d565-5842-972a-4024fc2947d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 11 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f078de7-6bf2-52c5-bbfd-2095ee4f5399" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394301} :answer-field/id #uuid "8055db6f-b8ff-53ba-9dfb-f141126526ea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394298}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62dadad6-bc72-59c3-b335-faab5922669e" :question/math-academy-id "q-81847" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to S}$ from $B$ to $S$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors of $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 11\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b973d79-8fe4-5033-8202-9693004855dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix When One of the Bases is the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "026f1607-4c08-5aa9-bf58-cfb40b330832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=0,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394306} {:answer/id #uuid "3baad480-b3b5-5d93-a51a-4fd762b5a712" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=7,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394307} {:answer/id #uuid "d0c36859-9ec2-5784-9edb-98a495812bcc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394308} {:answer/id #uuid "43bfde7c-5a27-5e7c-9c33-3a11017707bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=0,$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394309} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe87549-d159-56b7-9c56-ba13c3e649d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=0,$ $b=3,$ and $c=-3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394310}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "026f1607-4c08-5aa9-bf58-cfb40b330832" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=7,$ $b=0,$ and $c=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394306} :answer-field/id #uuid "904c9209-1445-5996-996f-3111827c9049" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38cd5032-0982-5775-aa3b-320af45a4643" :question/math-academy-id "q-81848" :question/problem "Let $B={{b}_{1},{b}_{2},{b}_{3}}$ be a basis of ${R}^{3}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What are the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & a & -3 \\\\ b & -2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=7,$ $b=0,$ and $c=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394311 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb6b89d9-5ecc-523c-9fe0-a98270e715c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394313} {:answer/id #uuid "b48cca5a-57d5-5e3e-85fc-5f7371b9a168" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394314} {:answer/id #uuid "ebadf3ae-54e1-5e2d-ac3c-70c2f31c63ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394315} {:answer/id #uuid "0f308fd4-f8a6-5e5f-860e-48792c8fbbdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394316} {:answer/id #uuid "00d59288-f8b5-5054-8c1f-b5518246d305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394317}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00d59288-f8b5-5054-8c1f-b5518246d305" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394317} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d1bd593-f01f-5378-b1fe-6d41f9f01e52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394312}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a27a3c1e-ad3c-56e4-aa1a-d2aedbc7058d" :question/math-academy-id "q-81859" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-9)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394318 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "23b6a85e-e1de-590c-84d2-379052b1b9f3" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Invertibility Property of the Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceb0e647-cc62-5520-96d0-a62ea4b7b70a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394320} {:answer/id #uuid "91a2a780-e072-57b3-a258-6f82c21f34c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ 9 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394321} {:answer/id #uuid "b5dc2ed0-ef85-5f46-8701-75a045762a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394322} {:answer/id #uuid "64865972-6c7f-5e6a-914f-7ca5dabf44da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394323} {:answer/id #uuid "e7efc8a7-50e9-5361-a425-938758af899c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64865972-6c7f-5e6a-914f-7ca5dabf44da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394323} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ec1a78f-b951-5ee0-943b-80972e450245" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394319}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3af65f58-119f-54f0-83bf-1e885de9ae50" :question/math-academy-id "q-81860" :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-7)-9\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 9 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9c85b7b-1326-5f25-aa21-ab115680582d" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Statement Using De Morgan's Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d0174c-216d-5a1c-b294-48f11936eab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬A∨¬B)∨(¬A∧B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394326} {:answer/id #uuid "c30b5ec0-9348-534b-b1c7-70a58cd4c300" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬A∨¬B)∧(¬A∧B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394327} {:answer/id #uuid "1e7a33a2-8d59-556b-ad78-6f74cf92b632" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬A∧¬B)∨(¬A∧B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394328} {:answer/id #uuid "07267292-6e72-56ac-b75b-e3dedff0c29b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∨¬B)∧(¬A∧B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394329} {:answer/id #uuid "9be95572-50bc-59d3-96f2-c8a03f4f753d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(A∨¬B)∨(¬A∧B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394330}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85d0174c-216d-5a1c-b294-48f11936eab0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(¬A∨¬B)∨(¬A∧B)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394326} :answer-field/id #uuid "db164e91-e5c4-5e47-9404-67178bbb6a5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1738f436-f695-5752-b705-3c9dc3e58972" :question/math-academy-id "q-81865" :question/problem "Rewrite the statement $¬((A∧B)∧(A∨¬B))$ using De Morgan's laws." :question/worked-solution "De Morgan's laws state that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬(A∧B)≡¬A∨¬B, \\\\ ¬(A∨B)≡¬A∧¬B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we can simplify the given statement as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}¬((A∧B)∧(A∨¬B)) & ≡¬(A∧B)∨¬(A∨¬B) \\\\  & ≡(¬A∨¬B)∨(¬A∧¬(¬B)) \\\\  & ≡(¬A∨¬B)∨(¬A∧B)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eec6cf11-74cc-5b3a-b2fe-bd4285fe4a88" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting Conditional Statement Using the Associative, Commutative, and Absorption Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "911a45b8-ba13-5065-bb06-c450effaabf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394332} {:answer/id #uuid "95480722-7569-5083-8b58-56b83301d195" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394333} {:answer/id #uuid "8476685e-e104-531e-86f2-c09e6663f600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "¬X∨Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394334} {:answer/id #uuid "eee8dbbe-fc4d-5c20-9c21-6ae1d839c1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394335} {:answer/id #uuid "77a3543d-dc9d-5f25-b6e9-2b5f550e3919" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "X∧¬Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394336}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eee8dbbe-fc4d-5c20-9c21-6ae1d839c1fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394335} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6f0c2ff-63c9-5651-8370-33bd7ba743eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186394331}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0467eb40-db5b-5041-af5a-7039ef1e5d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-81891" :question/problem "Simplify the statement $¬(X∧Y)⇒Y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that\n\n$$\n¬(X∧Y)⇒Y≡¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y.\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n¬(¬(X∧Y))∨Y≡(X∧Y)∨Y.\n$$\n\nFinally, by the absorption law, we have\n\n$$\n(X∧Y)∨Y≡Y.\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $Y.$"}]}
